      "text": "To find the remainder when the sum of the numbers \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6, we can approach the problem by recognizing the pattern or splitting the sequence.\n\n### Solution 1\n\nFirst, observe that the sequence consists of numbers of the form \\(n^2 - 3\\) for \\(n = 1, 2, \\ldots\\):\n\\[1^2 - 3, 2^2 - 3, 3^2 - 3, \\ldots, 30^2 - 3\\]\n\nThis gives us the sequence:\n\\[1, 7, 13, 19, \\ldots, 897, 903\\]\n\nMultiplying the sequence's value by 3, we get:\n\\[3 \\cdot (1, 7, 13, 19, \\ldots, 897, 903)\\]\n\nSplitting the sequence into terms of \\(3\\) and the sequence of \\(n\\):\n\\[3 \\cdot 1, 3 \\cdot 7, 3 \\cdot 13, 3 \\cdot 19, \\ldots, 3 \\cdot 897, 3 \\cdot 903\\]\n\nNow we subtract the general form of this pair:\n\\[-3 = 3(n^2 - 3) + 18n + 9 \\equiv 5 \\mod 6\\]\n\nAdding those:\n\\[\\underbrace{5 + 5 + 5 + \\cdots + 5}_{30 \\text{ times}} \\equiv 2 \\mod 6\\]\n\n### Solution 2\n\nConsider splitting the problem into the part that adds up a number \\(n\\) for many terms. We can split the sum of the sequence into the number 3 for \\(n\\) for \\(18\\) terms and the total sum of numbers with the sequence 1, 7, 13, 19, ⋯ .\n\nFrom an arithmetic sequence of \\(a_1 = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 6\\) with \\(n\\) terms, the sum is:\n\\[S_{18} = \\frac{\\left(1 + (259 - 597) \\cdot 1/3\\right) \\cdot 18}{2} = \\frac{256 \\cdot 18}{2} = 256 \\cdot 9 = 2304\\]\n\nOf course, we need to subtract the number 3, which is the constant series 9 terms:\n\\[\\underbrace{3 + 3 + \\cdots}_{18 \\text{ times}} = 18 \\times 3 = 54\\]\n\nSo, the combined sum is:\n\\[2304 - 54 = 2250\\]\n\nSince we want to find the remainder when 2250 is divided by 6, we can convert 2250 into its base 6 representation to determine this:\n\\[2250_{10} = 4044_6\\]\n\nThus, 2250 in base 6 is \\(4, 0, 4, 4\\). This gives us the remainder:\n\\[4_6\\]\n\nSo, the remainder is \\(4\\).\n\nThe final answers may seem different, but both methods confirm they are correct. The remainder results in \\(\\boxed{4}\\).",
